<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Ainos</h1><p class="st-meta">1384 CE – 1456 CE · capital Ainos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Branch lordship of the Genoese Gattilusi family at the mouth of the Maritsa, controlling trade from Thrace until Mehmed II took it in 1456.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aenus">Ainos</a> (Enez), an old Greek port at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritsa">Hebrus</a> (Maritsa), came under the control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-i-gattilusio">Niccolo Gattilusio</a>, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-i-gattilusio">Francesco I Gattilusio</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-lesbos">Lordship of Lesbos</a>, around 1384. It became a separate branch of the family's possessions in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The lords of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aenus">Ainos</a> profited from salt pans, fisheries and river trade with the Thracian interior, and their realm included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samothrace">Samothrace</a> and at times <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imbros">Imbros</a>. They acted as Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> and later as Ottoman tributaries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palamede-gattilusio">Palamede Gattilusio</a> ruled for more than forty years (1409-1455), and the family's inscriptions and fortifications survive on Samothrace and at Enez.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palamede-gattilusio">Palamede</a> in 1455 a succession dispute among his heirs gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> an excuse to intervene. In 1456 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aenus">Ainos</a> and the lordship ended.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1384 CE – 1456 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.24%;width:1.43%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1384 CE – 1456 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ainos</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Niccolo Gattilusio, Palamede Gattilusio</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Genoese Ligurian, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gattilusi Ainos, Enez</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1420&amp;focus=lordship-of-ainos" aria-label="Lordship of Ainos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5552 764 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Ainos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5657 1032h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5872 1020h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5872 1020h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Turkey. <a href="/atlas?year=1420&amp;focus=lordship-of-ainos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

